var e=[{id:`fundamental-distributed-systems`,pathId:`distributed-systems`,order:0,title:`Distributed Systems — Overview`,subtitle:`"The network is reliable." "Latency is zero." "Bandwidth is infinite." Peter Deutsch's 1994 list of fallacies still names the bugs production teams ship today. The discipline of di`,minutes:13,difficulty:4.5,tags:[`CAP`,`FLP`,`consensus`,`failure modes`,`time`],story:{headline:`The night two regions both thought they were primary`,paragraphs:[`It's 14:00 during a blue-green cutover and the pager lights up: duplicate refunds in prod. Payments platform, two regions, one async replication link between them. A backbone maintenance window blips the inter-region link for ninety seconds. The failover controller does exactly what it was built to do — promotes Region B and starts sending writes there. What it doesn't do is tell Region A, whose health checks are still green on its side of the partition. For ninety seconds, both regions accept refunds as primary.`,`The reconciliation job catches the conflict an hour later — vector clocks flag the two refund records as concurrent writes — but the app's merge logic was written by someone who believed fallacy number one. It picks 'latest by timestamp,' and the two regions' clocks disagree by 180 milliseconds. The 'later' refund wins twice. A customer's balance goes negative, and finance finds out before engineering does.`,`At 4 AM, over the incident bridge, someone asks the question that starts every distributed systems education: 'How can two machines both think they're primary?' The answer is the whole discipline: because the network is not reliable, latency is not zero, topology does change, and no, you cannot compare timestamps from two clocks. Peter Deutsch wrote all of this down in 1994. We shipped it anyway.`],problem:`Two regions both accepted refund writes during a 90-second partition; timestamp-based reconciliation picked the wrong winner. Which fallacies did we believe, and what does the discipline say to do instead?`},tech:{stack:[`CAP Theorem`,`PACELC`,`Lamport Clocks`,`Vector Clocks`,`Hybrid Logical Clocks`,`TrueTime`,`Raft Consensus`],note:`The theorems and clocks that decide who wins when two nodes disagree — from CAP's partition bargain to HLC's software-only TrueTime.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: the eight fallacies are your bug backlog`,body:[`Let's start where every incident starts: somebody believed a sentence that isn't true. Deutsch's 1994 list — network reliable, latency zero, bandwidth infinite, network secure, topology stable, one admin, transport cost free, network homogeneous — reads like trivia until you map each line to a page in your own incident log. 'The network is reliable' is your failover controller promoting a second primary. 'Latency is zero' is the cross-region call sitting in your hot path at 150 milliseconds a pop.`,`And here's the part nobody says out loud: 'why not just retry?' is itself a fallacy. Retry a non-idempotent refund and you've invented money. 'Bandwidth is infinite' shows up as an egress bill. 'One admin' shows up the day team A's change window collides with team B's. The discipline isn't memorising the list — it's running your design against it like a linter.`,`So when you review any distributed design, ask one question per fallacy: what happens the day this sentence is false? If you can't answer, you don't have a design — you have a hope.`],quote:`Every distributed bug you'll ever ship is one of these eight sentences wearing a costume.`,table:{headers:[`Fallacy`,`What it looks like in prod`,`What you do instead`],rows:[[`Network is reliable`,`Failover drills never test the link; partitions happen daily at scale`,`Timeouts, retries with backoff, idempotency keys`],[`Latency is zero`,`Cross-region call in the hot path; p99 = 150ms+`,`Keep cross-region out of the request path`],[`Bandwidth is infinite`,`Full-table dumps between regions; egress bill shock`,`Ship deltas, compress, batch`],[`Network is secure`,`Plaintext internal traffic 'because it's inside'`,`mTLS everywhere; assume MITM`],[`Topology doesn't change`,`Hardcoded IPs; pods and DNS churn constantly`,`Service discovery, low DNS TTLs`],[`One administrator`,`Two teams, two clouds, two change policies`,`Explicit contracts, shared runbooks`],[`Transport cost is zero`,`Serialisation + encryption CPU ignored in sizing`,`Measure codec/TLS CPU; budget it`],[`Network is homogeneous`,`Same code path for 5G, Wi-Fi, satellite links`,`Test worst-case MTU and latency per link`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Distributed Systems — Overview`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-distributed-systems.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: CAP and PACELC`,body:[`CAP says something very narrow and very brutal: during a partition, you pick. Consistency means refuse writes you can't order — stay linearisable. Availability means accept them and reconcile later. You cannot have both, because the partition has cut the quorum in half and both halves think they're right. Note the fine print: the C is linearisability. Eventually consistent systems aren't 'violating CAP' — weaker consistency just doesn't trigger the trade.`,`But CAP only speaks when the network is down, which is like a seatbelt law that only applies during crashes. PACELC fills the gap: else — in normal operation — you pick latency or consistency. Sync quorum writes are slower; async replication is fast but eventual. Cassandra is PA/EL: available under partition, latency-first when healthy. HBase is PC/EC. MongoDB is PA/EC. Dynamo chose A; Spanner chose C — and paid for it with atomic clocks, which we'll get to.`,`The practical move: most modern stores let you tune this per request. Your payment write and your analytics write should not have the same answer on this tree.`],quote:`CAP isn't a config flag — it's a decision you make every time the link drops.`,table:{headers:[`System`,`PACELC position`,`What it means for you`],rows:[[`Cassandra`,`PA/EL`,`Available under partition; latency over consistency when healthy`],[`HBase`,`PC/EC`,`Consistency under partition and in normal operation`],[`MongoDB`,`PA/EC`,`Available under partition; consistent when healthy`],[`Dynamo-style`,`PA/EL`,`Accept writes always; reconcile with vector clocks`],[`Spanner`,`PC/EC`,`Refuse or wait rather than diverge; TrueTime bounds the wait`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Distributed Systems — Overview`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-distributed-systems.html`}},{heading:`03 · The hard one: time`,body:[`In one process, time is monotonic and boring. Across nodes, every clock disagrees — NTP keeps them within milliseconds on a good day, and 'a good day' is doing a lot of work in that sentence. So you cannot order events on different machines by wall clock without ambiguity, and our 3 AM merge logic just learned that the expensive way.`,`The fixes are logical, not physical. Lamport clocks: a counter per node, bumped on every event, attached to every message; on receive you take max(local, received) plus one. That gives you happens-before for causally related events — and says nothing about concurrent ones. Vector clocks go further: a counter per node for every node, so you can detect concurrency and hand the client the data to merge. That's what Dynamo did, and what our reconciliation job flagged.`,`Then there's the cheating answer: Google's TrueTime returns 'now is between X and Y' from atomic clocks and GPS, and Spanner simply waits out the uncertainty to give you global ordering. HLC — hybrid logical clocks — is the software-only approximation: wall clock plus a logical tiebreak, close to physical time but skew-corrected. CockroachDB and YugabyteDB run on these.`],quote:`A timestamp is a claim, not a fact — two clocks never agree enough to settle an argument.`,table:{headers:[`Clock`,`What it tells you`,`Cost`],rows:[[`Wall clock (NTP)`,`Approximate time; ms-to-seconds skew`,`Cheap; ambiguous ordering`],[`Lamport clock`,`Causal happens-before order`,`One counter + one message field`],[`Vector clock`,`Detects concurrent writes`,`O(nodes) per message`],[`TrueTime`,`Bounded interval: now is X to Y`,`Atomic clocks + GPS + commit wait`],[`HLC`,`Physical time + logical tiebreak`,`Software only; CockroachDB, YugabyteDB`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Distributed Systems — Overview`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-distributed-systems.html`}},{heading:`04 · Why consensus costs: FLP and the timeout tax`,body:[`Why can't we just have a correct, always-available consensus algorithm and stop talking? Because FLP — Fischer, Lynch, Paterson, 1985 — proved you can't: in a fully asynchronous system, no deterministic consensus algorithm guarantees termination with even one crash fault. Not 'hard' — impossible. Every real system cheats the same way: timeouts. Paxos and Raft live in partial synchrony; the leader lease expires, someone calls an election, and progress resumes. That's also why blips cause false leader changes — the timeout tax.`,`The same trade shows up in the vocabulary. ACID is the single-machine contract — transactions, no reconciliation, and one machine's availability ceiling. BASE is the distributed one — basically available, soft state, eventually consistent — and the bill arrives as merge logic your application now owns. A Raft quorum write buys linearisability at the cost of a majority round trip per write and 2f+1 nodes to survive f failures. Most data doesn't need that. Some data absolutely does. Knowing which is the job.`],quote:`FLP says agreement can't be free — timeouts are the tax every real consensus system pays.`,table:{headers:[`Guarantee`,`What it buys`,`What it costs`],rows:[[`ACID (single node)`,`Transactions, no reconciliation`,`One machine's availability ceiling`],[`BASE`,`Availability under partition`,`App-owned merge logic; eventual reads`],[`Raft/Paxos quorum`,`Linearisable writes, leader safety`,`Majority round trip per write; 2f+1 nodes for f faults`],[`Timeouts (partial synchrony)`,`Progress despite FLP`,`False leader changes during blips`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Distributed Systems — Overview`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-distributed-systems.html`}},{heading:`05 · Last thought: what to actually build`,body:[`So what do we actually build? Start single-machine. Seriously — most products never need real distribution, and every node you add buys you the whole tax table above. Add a read replica when reads saturate. Add multi-region when users or compliance demand it. Each step is a measured decision, not a resume line.`,`And when you do distribute: pick a CAP position per operation, design the reconciliation path before the first incident — vector clocks plus merge logic you've actually tested — and never let two clocks settle an argument. That's the whole discipline in one breath.`],quote:`Distributed systems is the art of pretending the network exists, while knowing it does.`,table:{headers:[`Signal`,`Do you need distribution?`],rows:[[`Single box handles p99 with headroom`,`No — stay single-machine`],[`Write volume exceeds one disk's fsync rate`,`Maybe — start with read replicas`],[`Users on multiple continents need low latency`,`Yes — but replicate reads, not necessarily writes`],[`You must survive a datacenter loss`,`Yes — and now you must pick a CAP position`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Distributed Systems — Overview`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-distributed-systems.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`split-brain-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Split-brain: the fallacies at 3 AM`,caption:`A 90-second link blip promotes Region B while Region A still thinks it's primary — both write, vector clocks flag the conflict, and timestamp merge picks the wrong winner.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    C[Client] --> LB[Global load balancer]
    LB -->|healthy link| RA[Region A - accepts writes]
    LB -.->|failover| RB[Region B - also accepts writes]
    RA <-->|async replication| RB
    RA --> RC[Reconciliation job]
    RB --> RC
    RC --> VC[Vector clocks flag concurrent writes]
    VC --> MW[App merge: latest timestamp wins]
    MW --> L[Wrong winner - balance goes negative]`},{id:`pacelc-decision`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The PACELC decision tree`,caption:`Every write walks this tree: under a partition pick consistency or availability; in normal operation pick latency or consistency. Most databases let you choose per request.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    W[Write arrives] --> P{Partition right now?}
    P -->|Yes| CAP{Pick one}
    CAP -->|Consistency| RJ[Reject writes - stay linearisable]
    CAP -->|Availability| AW[Accept writes - reconcile later]
    P -->|No| EL{Pick one}
    EL -->|Latency| AS[Async replication - fast but eventual]
    EL -->|Consistency| Q[Sync quorum - slower but strong]`}],takeaways:[`Budget 100–200ms RTT for any cross-region hop; if your p99 needs a cross-region round trip per write, redesign before you scale.`,`Assume at least one partition per month at scale — design the reconciliation path before the first incident, not after.`,`Never order events across nodes by wall clock; NTP drift of tens of milliseconds is normal — use Lamport, vector, or HLC clocks, or accept last-write-wins losses.`,`Pick consistency per operation, not per database: quorum writes for payments, eventual for analytics — most stores let you tune per request.`,`Start single-machine and add distribution only when a measured workload demands it — most products never need real distribution.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Distributed Systems — Overview`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-distributed-systems.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`mj-F7nnCsIsVi0`,note:`Mastering Distributed Tracing with Serilog and Seq in .NET covers this from a different angle.`},{id:`mj-SA8BainHeS0`,note:`How To Implement Distributed Tracing for Microservices With Jaeger covers this from a different angle.`},{id:`mj-191CJFrvBxM`,note:`Distributed Systems Made Easy: URL Shortener Architecture with .NET Aspire covers this from a different angle.`}],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-concurrency-vs-parallelism`,pathId:`distributed-systems`,order:1,title:`Concurrency vs Parallelism`,subtitle:`Rob Pike's distinction is one of the most useful sentences in the field. Concurrency is structure: many independent things in flight. Parallelism is execution: many things running `,minutes:8,difficulty:3.5,tags:[`Async/await`,`cores`,`goroutines`,`threads`],story:{headline:`Twelve thousand connections, one busy core`,paragraphs:[`11:45 when batch reconciliation started, the Saturday before Black Friday. The checkout API's p99 goes from 80 ms to 9 seconds in eleven minutes. Maya, on call, does the reflex dance: restart, watch it recover, watch it fall over again. The dashboards show 12,000 open connections — the service is accepting every one of them, happily. Nothing is down. Everything is slow.`,`She finally pulls the CPU graph and stares: core 0 flatlined at 100%, cores 1 through 7 dead idle. The team had spent the whole quarter 'making everything async' — every handler awaited, every call non-blocking. And it worked, as structure: thousands of requests in flight, none dropped. But every one of them funneled through a single event loop on a single core, and somewhere in that loop sat a synchronous image-resize burning 40 ms of pure CPU per request. Twelve thousand concurrent requests. One core doing the work. Concurrent, not parallel.`,`The fix took an afternoon: move the resize onto worker threads and run the cluster across all eight cores. Same code structure, same async handlers — but now execution actually overlapped. p99 landed at 90 ms and stayed there through Black Friday. The postmortem's title was Rob Pike's line, because it was the entire story: the team had built a system doing many things, and assumed that meant many things at once.`],problem:`Why can a service hold thousands of simultaneous connections and still crawl — and why doesn't 'making it async' fix it?`},tech:{stack:[`Event Loop`,`async/await`,`goroutines`,`OS Threads`,`Worker Threads`,`SIMD`],note:`The structures that keep many tasks in flight (event loop, async/await, goroutines, OS threads) versus the engines that actually run tasks side by side (worker threads, SIMD). The whole lesson lives in the gap between those two lists.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why didn't async save us?`,body:[`Let's start with the trap, because everyone falls into it. The team 'went async' and expected speed. Async/await is a concurrency tool — it lets one thread hold thousands of tasks in flight by suspending them at await points instead of burning a whole thread per wait. That's a genuine structural win: no thread-per-request memory blowup, no dropped connections under load.`,`But waiting is not working. When a request hits the network, suspending it is free — the CPU was idle anyway. When a request needs 40 ms of actual computation, there's nothing to await: the loop must run it, and while it runs, nothing else in the process does. Async put more requests in the queue; it never hired a second cashier.`,`That's the whole incident in one sentence. Concurrency is how many things are in flight. Parallelism is how many run at the same instant. The fix wasn't more async — it was giving the concurrent structure somewhere to actually execute.`],quote:`Async put more requests in line. It never hired a second cashier.`,table:{headers:[`Attempt at 3 AM`,`What it actually changed`,`Result`],rows:[[`Restart the service`,`Cleared queues, same structure`,`Fell over again in 10 min`],[`Raise connection pool 50 to 200`,`More in-flight I/O waits`,`No change — CPU is the wall`],[`Async/await everywhere`,`More concurrency, still one core`,`Slightly worse (scheduling overhead)`],[`Move resize to worker threads`,`Actual parallel execution`,`p99 9 s to 90 ms`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Concurrency vs Parallelism`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-concurrency-vs-parallelism.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the four quadrants`,body:[`Here's the map I draw on every whiteboard. Two axes: is there concurrent structure — many independent things in flight? And is there parallel execution — many things running on different cores at the same wall-clock moment? Four quadrants, and every system you'll ever debug lives in one of them.`,`A Node server on one core with 5,000 open connections: concurrent, not parallel. A BLAS matrix multiply or a GPU shader: parallel, not concurrent — one task split across lanes, no interleaving structure to speak of. A Go web server: both. A bash script: neither. The quadrants aren't rankings; they're diagnoses.`,`The important edge cases are the off-diagonals. Concurrent-without-parallel is perfectly fine when work is I/O-bound — waiting dominates, one core suffices. Parallel-without-concurrent is fine when one task is big and divisible. Trouble starts the moment you assume one implies the other.`],quote:`Concurrency enables parallelism. It doesn't require it — and neither implies the other.`,table:{headers:[`State`,`What it looks like`,`Example`],rows:[[`Neither`,`One thing at a time`,`Bash script`],[`Concurrent, not parallel`,`Many in flight, one core`,`Node.js, asyncio on 1 CPU`],[`Parallel, not concurrent`,`One task across many cores`,`NumPy / BLAS, GPU shader`],[`Both`,`Many tasks across many cores`,`Go web server, Erlang, modern .NET`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Concurrency vs Parallelism`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-concurrency-vs-parallelism.html`}},{heading:`03 · Picking a structure: how languages hold many things in flight`,body:[`So how do you express 'many things in flight'? Every language community picked a different answer, and every trade-off comes down to one number: cost per in-flight task.`,`OS threads: kernel-scheduled, roughly a megabyte of stack apiece, fine at thousands, painful at a hundred thousand. Event loops: one thread, dirt cheap per task, but any CPU-bound work freezes everything. Goroutines and JVM 21+ virtual threads: runtime-managed, a few kilobytes each, scale into the millions — and you write plain blocking-looking code while the runtime multiplexes onto a handful of OS threads. Actors: concurrency as isolated state machines passing messages — structure you can reason about, at the price of indirection.`,`My rule: pick by in-flight count and blocking style. Under ~10k concurrent waits, threads are fine and simplest. Millions of lightweight tasks, or code that wants to look synchronous? Goroutines or virtual threads. Single-threaded simplicity with pure I/O? Event loop — but only if nothing ever computes on it.`],quote:`The goroutine bargain: write blocking-looking code, let the runtime multiplex it onto a few real threads.`,table:{headers:[`Model`,`Cost per task`,`Practical ceiling`,`Watch out for`],rows:[[`OS threads`,`~1 MB stack`,`thousands per process`,`memory + context-switch blowup`],[`Event loop`,`~bytes`,`100k+ connections`,`one CPU task blocks everything`],[`async/await`,`~bytes`,`100k+ per loop`,`needs multi-threaded runtime for parallelism`],[`goroutines / virtual threads`,`~2-8 KB`,`millions per process`,`scheduling hidden in the runtime`],[`Actors`,`mailbox per actor`,`millions`,`indirection, message ordering`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Concurrency vs Parallelism`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-concurrency-vs-parallelism.html`}},{heading:`04 · Picking an engine: where parallelism actually comes from`,body:[`Now the other axis: where does simultaneous execution physically come from? Four engines, ordered by how close the parallelism sits to the silicon.`,`SIMD/vector: one instruction applied to many data points — AVX, NEON, GPU lanes. Compilers auto-vectorize simple loops; huge wins for matrix math and image work, which is exactly the workload that hurt us. Multi-core SMP: threads or workers pinned across cores — the default story, and the moment your bottleneck quietly shifts from compute to memory bandwidth and lock contention. GPU: thousands of simple cores; enormous for embarrassingly-parallel problems, but the algorithm has to fit the architecture. Distributed: parallelism across machines — MapReduce, Spark — where the network becomes the bottleneck and it's a different design problem entirely.`,`Notice the pattern: every engine buys speed by moving the bottleneck somewhere else. Compute, then memory, then interconnect, then network. Choosing an engine means choosing which wall you'd rather be standing against.`],quote:`Every parallelism engine just relocates the bottleneck — compute, memory, or the network. Pick your wall.`,table:{headers:[`Engine`,`Granularity`,`Best for`,`New bottleneck`],rows:[[`SIMD / vector`,`one instruction, many data`,`matrix math, imaging, scanning`,`memory bandwidth`],[`Multi-core SMP`,`task per core`,`general services`,`locks + cache coherence`],[`GPU`,`thousands of light threads`,`rendering, ML`,`data transfer + programming model`],[`Distributed`,`task per machine`,`huge batch jobs`,`the network`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Concurrency vs Parallelism`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-concurrency-vs-parallelism.html`}},{heading:`05 · The design decision: structure first, speed second`,body:[`So here's the decision procedure I actually use, and it's two questions in order. First: how many independent things does this system need in flight? That's a design question — it decides your structure, your concurrency model, your backpressure. Second: how many of those things must run at the same instant to hit the latency target? That's an implementation question — it decides workers, cores, vectorization.`,`For an I/O-bound API — most web services — the answer to question two is 'barely any': requests spend their lives waiting on the network and the database, so concurrency without much parallelism is the right shape. For a CPU-bound transform — our image resize — question two is the whole game, and no amount of async substitutes for more executors.`,`The failure mode is answering question two while ignoring question one, or vice versa. Maya's team spent a quarter on question one and never asked question two. Get them in order and the rest is plumbing.`],quote:`Concurrency is a design choice. Parallelism is an implementation detail. Decide them in that order.`,table:{headers:[`Workload`,`Design for`,`Why`],rows:[[`I/O-bound API`,`Concurrency: async/await, goroutines`,`life is spent waiting, not computing`],[`CPU-bound transform`,`Parallelism: worker pool, SIMD, GPU`,`compute needs cores, not awaits`],[`Mixed workload`,`Concurrency + offload CPU to workers`,`keep the loop free, farm the math`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology-before-after`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Same structure, new execution model`,caption:`The event loop held 12k requests in flight on one core; worker threads gave the identical concurrent structure four places to actually execute.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph BEFORE["Before - concurrent, not parallel"]
    direction TB
    LB1["12,000 open connections"] --> EL["Single event loop<br/>core 0 at 100 percent"]
    EL --> RZ["sync image resize<br/>40 ms CPU each"]
    RZ --> DB1[("Postgres")]
    IDLE["cores 1-7 idle"]
  end
  subgraph AFTER["After - concurrent AND parallel"]
    direction TB
    LB2["12,000 open connections"] --> RT["runtime scheduler"]
    RT --> W1["worker 1<br/>core 0"]
    RT --> W2["worker 2<br/>core 1"]
    RT --> W3["worker 3<br/>core 2"]
    RT --> W4["worker 4<br/>core 3"]
    W1 --> DB2[("Postgres")]
    W2 --> DB2
    W3 --> DB2
    W4 --> DB2
  end`},{id:`seq-blocking-loop`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One blocking task poisons the loop`,caption:`A single 40 ms CPU-bound handler delays every unrelated request queued behind it — the tax of concurrency without parallelism.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant A as Request A - image resize
  participant L as Event loop - one core
  participant B as Request B - health check
  A->>L: POST /resize
  Note right of L: sync CPU work - loop blocked 40 ms
  B->>L: GET /health
  Note over B: queued - nothing else can run
  L-->>A: 200 OK at t=40 ms
  L-->>B: 200 OK at t=40 ms - 39 ms too late`}],takeaways:[`I/O-bound service? Design for concurrency (async/await, goroutines) — requests spend 95%+ of their life waiting on the network, so extra cores buy almost nothing.`,`One 50 ms CPU-bound task on an event loop adds ~50 ms of latency to every request queued behind it — farm CPU work to worker threads or processes.`,`OS threads cost ~1 MB of stack each (thousands per process); goroutines and JVM virtual threads cost ~2-8 KB (millions per process) — match the runtime to your in-flight count.`,`Before scaling anything, read the CPU graph: one core pegged at 100% with the rest idle means you're concurrent but not parallel — add workers, not connections.`,`Concurrency is a design choice, parallelism an implementation detail — decide the structure first, then choose where execution actually overlaps.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Concurrency vs Parallelism`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-concurrency-vs-parallelism.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-actor-model`,pathId:`distributed-systems`,order:2,title:`Actor Model`,subtitle:`Carl Hewitt formulated the actor model in 1973. Erlang built a phone system on it in 1986. PayPal ran a billion transactions/day on it in 2014 (see PayPal case). The primitive is u`,minutes:11,difficulty:4.5,tags:[`Akka.NET`,`Erlang/OTP`,`Orleans`,`Pony`],story:{headline:`Black Friday, one hot lock, and the race that didn't care.`,paragraphs:[`09:30 during a latency regression audit, Black Friday. The payments ledger's p99 goes from 12 ms to 9 seconds in four minutes. Thread dumps show 4,100 threads parked on a single synchronized block — the account-map lock guarding every balance read-modify-write in the service. On-call widens the pool, rolls the fleet, and the graph flattens. For eleven minutes.`,`Then the pager again: a merchant's balance is wrong by €1,200. The post-mortem finds it in an hour — a check-then-act race in a reconciliation path that read the balance outside the lock and wrote it back inside. The lock held perfectly. The race didn't care, because that path had simply forgotten to take it. The weekend task list: audit all 31 code paths that touch account state, each one a fresh chance to get the locking wrong.`,`The rewrite took six weeks and deleted the entire problem class: one actor per account. All 31 paths became messages in one mailbox, and the handler became the only code on Earth allowed to touch that balance. No locks to forget, no paths to audit — the serialization is the data structure. PayPal had already run a billion transactions a day on exactly this primitive back in 2014; after that weekend, we finally understood why.`],problem:`How do you serialize mutation across millions of mutable entities — without locks, deadlocks, or hand-auditing every code path that touches shared state?`},tech:{stack:[`Erlang/OTP`,`Akka.NET`,`Microsoft Orleans`,`Pony`,`Supervision Trees`,`Bounded Mailboxes`],note:`Erlang/OTP, Akka.NET, and Orleans are the production-grade actor runtimes this story lands on — mailboxes, supervision trees, and cluster sharding out of the box; Pony proves the same model with compile-time capabilities. Bounded mailboxes and supervision strategies are the production add-ons the tutorials skip.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just add a lock?`,body:[`When you first hear 'one handler, one message at a time,' the instinct is: I can do that with a lock. And you can — for about a week. A coarse lock serializes everything, so your 64-core box behaves like a 1-core box under load. A fine-grained lock gives you parallelism, then hands you a deadlock graph to solve at 3 AM. I have lived both, and the Black Friday story is what the coarse version looks like in production.`,`The actor move is categorically different: it doesn't protect state from concurrent access, it makes concurrent access impossible. The state lives inside the actor, the mailbox serializes arrivals, and the handler is the only code that ever touches the balance. You deleted the race; you didn't guard it. And you still get parallelism — the runtime schedules thousands of actors across a small thread pool. Concurrency between actors is the whole game; concurrency inside an actor simply doesn't exist.`,`The cost is honesty. Everything is async: no direct calls, no shared memory, and replies are messages too, addressed back to a reply-to. That's a real tax on trivial CRUD. But for state that mutates — balances, sessions, carts — you pay for serialization one way or another. Actors make you pay it explicitly, in message design, instead of implicitly, in lock bugs.`],quote:`You can't have a data race on state only one handler can touch.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Coarse lock`,`Simple to reason about`,`Serializes everything; contention storms`,`Low traffic only`],[`Fine-grained locks`,`Real parallelism`,`Deadlocks; every path must comply`,`Expert-only`],[`STM / optimistic`,`No deadlocks`,`Retry storms under contention`,`Read-heavy workloads`],[`Actor per entity`,`No locks; natural sharding`,`Async everywhere; no cross-entity txns`,`Entity-centric state`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Actor Model`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-actor-model.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the mailbox: the queue that quietly owns your p99`,body:[`The mailbox looks like a boring implementation detail. It isn't — it's your backpressure story. FIFO is guaranteed per sender-receiver pair; ordering across different senders is undefined, so never build logic that assumes the world's messages arrive in world order. And send is fire-and-forget: it returns before the receiver has even seen the message. Need an answer? The receiver sends one back to your reply-to address — a message, not a method return.`,`Default mailboxes are unbounded, and that's the trap. A slow consumer plus a fast producer means the queue grows until the heap dies, and your dashboards look fine right up until they very suddenly don't. The production answer: bound it, and choose what happens at the edge — drop-new with a metric, backpressure to the producer, or fail-fast so the caller sees the truth.`,`And the classic anti-pattern: blocking inside a handler waiting for a reply deadlocks the actor, because the reply can only be processed by the handler that's currently stuck waiting for it. Ask with a timeout, off the handler's context — or better, tell and handle the reply as just another message.`],quote:`An unbounded mailbox is a memory leak with a queue's manners.`,table:{headers:[`Mailbox policy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Unbounded`,`Never drops a message`,`OOM under runaway producers`,`Never in production`],[`Bounded + drop-new`,`Protects memory`,`Silent loss unless instrumented`,`Telemetry, dedupe-able events`],[`Bounded + backpressure`,`End-to-end flow control`,`Couples producer to consumer speed`,`Streams, pipelines`],[`Bounded + fail-fast`,`Loud and actionable`,`Caller sees errors`,`Payments, order paths`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Actor Model`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-actor-model.html`}},{heading:`03 · Let it crash: supervision is your error handler`,body:[`Erlang's contribution isn't just the actor — it's what happens when one fails. Don't write defensive code for bugs you couldn't anticipate; you can't, by definition. Let the actor crash. Its supervisor — a parent actor with a strategy — restarts it: one-for-one (just the crashed child), one-for-all (all children, when they share state), or rest-for-one (the crashed child plus later siblings, when startup order matters).`,`This sounds reckless until you compare it to the alternative: a half-updated actor limping along, corrupting state quietly. A clean crash plus a restart from a known snapshot is more correct than any catch block you were going to write. The track record is real — Ericsson's AXD301 switch built on this model carried nine-nines availability claims, and WhatsApp pushed over two million TCP connections through a single Erlang node. Crash-only design has been carrying production traffic since before most of us were writing code.`,`The discipline that makes it work: restarts need budgets. If a child crashes ten times in a minute, the supervisor should stop restarting and escalate. A restart storm is just a crash with extra steps — and a pager is the correct output.`],quote:`Bugs you didn't anticipate are bugs you can't defend against — so make the crash the defense.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Behavior`,`Use when`,`Risk`],rows:[[`One-for-one`,`Restart only the crashed child`,`Failures are independent`,`Correlated failures cause restart storms`],[`One-for-all`,`Restart every child`,`Children share state`,`Amplifies a single failure`],[`Rest-for-one`,`Restart crashed child + later siblings`,`Startup order matters`,`Slightly harder to reason about`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Actor Model`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-actor-model.html`}},{heading:`04 · Location transparency: same address, slower wire`,body:[`An actor reference looks identical whether the actor lives in your process, on another machine in the cluster, or in another datacenter. You send the message; the runtime routes it. That's why Akka Cluster, Orleans, and Erlang's distributed mode feel coherent — you design as if everything were local, and the runtime handles placement and the wire.`,`But transparency hides the wire, not the physics. In-process send is nanoseconds; cross-node is milliseconds plus serialization plus failure modes that don't exist locally. The rule that saves teams: keep the API identical, but keep the conversation short. Two actors chatting across the network turn every business operation into a latency chain — batch instead of chat.`,`Delivery semantics stay humble everywhere: at-most-once, ordering per sender-receiver pair, nothing more. That means handlers should be idempotent and retries explicit. If you need guaranteed delivery, you build it deliberately on top — acks, retries, dedupe — rather than pretending the runtime gave it to you.`],quote:`Transparency hides the wire, not the physics.`,table:{headers:[`Dimension`,`In-process`,`Cross-node`,`Design rule`],rows:[[`Latency`,`Nanoseconds`,`Milliseconds + serialization`,`Batch, don't chat`],[`Failure modes`,`Same-process crashes`,`Partitions, timeouts`,`Model both from day one`],[`Ordering`,`Per-pair FIFO`,`Per-pair FIFO, nothing more`,`Never assume global order`],[`Delivery`,`At-most-once`,`At-most-once`,`Make handlers idempotent`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Actor Model`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-actor-model.html`}},{heading:`05 · Knowing when actors don't fit`,body:[`The sweet spot is one actor per real-world thing: account, device, session, order, game room, IoT sensor. State and behavior colocated, identity natural, sharding free. PayPal reported a billion transactions a day on eight VMs where forty used to stand; Orleans calls these grains and activates them on demand across a cluster, so a million entities doesn't mean a million threads — it means a million mailboxes on a dozen cores.`,`Where it doesn't fit: stateless CRUD (you're paying the async tax for nothing), bulk analytics scans (actors are terrible at fan-in reads), and anything needing a transaction across multiple entities. A two-phase commit across actors is pain; the honest answer is a saga or an orchestrator, not pretending it's one transaction.`,`The rule of thumb I give teams: if your hardest bug this quarter was a race on shared state, actors will pay for themselves within a month. If your hardest bug was a slow SQL query, they won't — and adding them will make the slow query harder to debug.`],quote:`Actors are a hammer for state that lives somewhere — don't swing them at state that lives nowhere.`,table:{headers:[`Workload`,`Actor fit`,`Why`],rows:[[`One entity per user/device/order`,`Excellent`,`State and behavior colocated`],[`IoT, sessions, games`,`Excellent`,`Millions of tiny state machines`],[`Stateless CRUD API`,`Poor`,`Async tax with no payoff`],[`Cross-entity transactions`,`Poor without care`,`Use sagas, not pretend-2PC`],[`Analytics / bulk scans`,`Poor`,`Actors aren't built for fan-in reads`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`actor-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One actor per entity, supervised`,caption:`The topology PayPal-style payment systems converge on: a shard region routes by account key, each actor owns its mailbox and private state, and a supervisor watches every child with a restart strategy.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
  Client[Client] --> GW[Gateway Actor]
  GW --> SR[Shard Region Router]
  subgraph Cluster["Actor cluster - one actor per account"]
    SUP["Supervisor - one-for-one"]
    A1["Account Actor 42<br/>mailbox + private state"]
    A2["Account Actor 7<br/>mailbox + private state"]
    A3["Account Actor 913<br/>mailbox + private state"]
    SUP -. watches and restarts .-> A1
    SUP -. watches and restarts .-> A2
    SUP -. watches and restarts .-> A3
  end
  SR --> A1
  SR --> A2
  SR --> A3
  A1 --> PS[(Snapshot store)]
  A2 --> PS`},{id:`mailbox-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Inside one mailbox: serialize, crash, restart`,caption:`Send is fire-and-forget, the handler processes one message at a time against private state, and a mid-stream crash is answered by the supervisor restarting the actor — the mailbox keeps its pending messages (Akka/Orleans semantics) and the fresh instance carries on.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  participant S as Sender actor
  participant M as Mailbox FIFO
  participant H as Account handler
  participant ST as Private state
  participant SUP as Supervisor
  S->>M: Deposit 50 - send returns instantly
  S->>M: Withdraw 30
  Note over M: bounded in prod - reject or backpressure when full
  M->>H: dequeue Deposit
  H->>ST: read-modify-write balance
  H-->>S: ack to reply-to address
  M->>H: dequeue Withdraw
  H->>H: handler bug - throws
  H--xSUP: crash signal
  SUP->>H: restart actor, reload from snapshot
  M->>H: next message hits fresh instance
  H-->>S: ack`}],takeaways:[`**One actor per real-world entity.** Account, device, session, order — Orleans grains and Akka Cluster shard millions of these across a cluster; size by entity count, not thread count.`,`**Bound every mailbox before you ship.** Pick a capacity and a policy — drop-new, backpressure, or fail-fast; an unbounded queue is an OOM waiting for your first slow consumer.`,`**Keep handlers to single-digit milliseconds.** While one message runs, the rest queue — the mailbox tail is your p99, so batch or offload anything slow.`,`**Never block inside a handler.** Use tell-and-reply-to, or ask with a timeout off the actor's context; one blocking ask can deadlock the actor waiting on the reply it can never process.`,`**Give supervision a budget.** One-for-one for independent children, one-for-all only when they share state, and escalate — stop restarting, page a human — after N crashes in M seconds.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Actor Model`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-actor-model.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-gossip`,pathId:`distributed-systems`,order:3,title:`Gossip Protocol`,subtitle:`Gossip protocols spread information through a cluster by random pairwise exchange. No central coordinator. Information reaches all nodes in O(log N) rounds. Cassandra, Consul, Serf`,minutes:9,difficulty:3.5,tags:[`Cassandra`,`Consul`,`SWIM`,`Serf`,`epidemic`],story:{headline:`The night our cluster quietly became two clusters.`,paragraphs:[`15:40 as connection pools saturated. Pager: 'Cassandra — repair inconsistencies on 6 of 12 nodes.' We'd just finished a network migration and a rolling restart. Every node's health check was green. nodetool status on node 1 showed 6 nodes Up and 6 nodes... absent. Not Down — absent, like they had never existed.`,`An hour of staring later, the answer: our automation had rewritten the seed list during the migration, and the new seed lists for the two racks didn't overlap. Gossip needs a bridge to cross — a node that knows both sides. With no shared seed, the cluster split into two perfect islands of six. Each island gossiped happily, converged flawlessly, served reads with local quorum, and reported itself fully healthy. Both halves were right. About themselves.`,`We added one overlapping seed, restarted, and watched the two epidemics merge in under ten seconds — membership counts snapped to 12 on every node. The protocol worked exactly as designed. That's the part that keeps you humble: gossip's failure mode isn't an error, it's confident, consistent disagreement.`],problem:`How do you tell a healthy gossip cluster from two healthy gossip clusters — and what does the protocol actually guarantee when the epidemic can't cross a gap?`},tech:{stack:[`SWIM`,`Phi Accrual`,`Push-Pull Anti-Entropy`,`Cassandra`,`Consul`,`Serf`],note:`Versioned per-key state, random-peer push-pull rounds, and SWIM-style indirect probing — the machinery that lets a cluster agree on who's alive with no coordinator in the middle.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just ask a master?`,body:[`Okay, so you've got N nodes and membership changes. Your first instinct as an engineer is: put a coordinator in charge. One service knows the truth, everyone asks it. And honestly — for a lot of clusters, that's the right call. etcd or Consul KV, done. The problem shows up when the coordinator sits on the critical path of every membership read and every join: now it's a single point of failure and a scaling bottleneck you didn't have before.`,`Option two: full mesh. Every node tells every other node. Beautifully simple, zero coordination, and O(N²) messages per change — at 1,000 nodes that's roughly a million messages for one node joining. It doesn't scale, full stop.`,`Option three is the one we're here for: gossip. Each node picks one random peer per round and they swap state. No coordinator to take down, O(1) messages per node per round, and the rumor still reaches everyone in log₂(N) rounds. The price you pay is eventual consistency on cluster state — two nodes can briefly disagree about who's alive. Ninety-nine percent of the time, that's a trade worth making.`],quote:`Gossip is the protocol that says: nobody knows, but everybody asks somebody, and the truth wins.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Central registry`,`Simple, one consistent view`,`SPOF, bottleneck on every join`,`< 50 nodes`],[`Full broadcast`,`No coordinator, instant spread`,`O(N²) messages per change`,`Tiny clusters only`],[`Gossip (SWIM-style)`,`O(1) msgs/node/round, no SPOF`,`Eventual consistency on membership`,`100s–1000s of nodes`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Gossip Protocol`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-gossip.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the round and the doubling math`,body:[`Here's the actual round, and it's almost embarrassingly simple. Every second or so, each node picks a random peer and they do a push-pull: A sends its state to B, B merges, B sends its state back to A, A merges. After one exchange, both ends agree on everything they've collectively seen.`,`The state itself is per-key versioned — 'my IP is 10.0.0.4, version 17.' The merge rule is one line: keep the higher version. Timestamps or a chosen tie-breaker for genuine conflicts. That's the entire conflict-resolution story, and it's enough.`,`Now the math, because it's the reason this protocol exists. One node learns a fact. Each round, roughly every informed node tells one new node — so the informed set roughly doubles: 1, 2, 4, 8... You hit log₂(N) rounds and everyone knows. For 1,000 nodes that's 10 rounds; at one gossip per second, ten seconds from 'one node knows' to 'all nodes know.' Total messages across the cluster: O(N log N) — the sweet spot between a coordinator's O(N) hot spot and broadcast's O(N²) blast.`],quote:`One rumor, one peer per round, and the informed set doubles — epidemics and information obey the same curve.`,table:{headers:[`Cluster size`,`Rounds to converge (log₂N)`,`Wall-clock @ 1 round/sec`],rows:[[`10`,`~4`,`~4 s`],[`100`,`~7`,`~7 s`],[`1,000`,`10`,`~10 s`],[`10,000`,`~14`,`~14 s`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Gossip Protocol`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-gossip.html`}},{heading:`03 · The SWIM trick: never trust one bad ping`,body:[`Membership spread is the easy half. The hard half is failure detection, and this is where naive designs bleed. The obvious approach — ping every peer every round — dies at scale. The lazy approach — ping one peer, mark dead on timeout — produces false corpses every time a network blips.`,`SWIM's trick is indirection. You ping one peer directly. If it doesn't ack, you don't declare it dead — you ask a few other random peers: 'hey, can you reach node 7?' If they can reach it, your own network path was the problem, and node 7 lives. If three independent peers all come back with 'can't reach it either,' that's not your blip — that's a consensus. Now you mark it suspect, gossip the suspicion, and promote it to dead after a timeout.`,`The insight I want you to keep: a single node's observation is noise. Multiple independent observers agreeing is signal. SWIM turns failure detection from 'my ping failed' into 'we all can't reach it,' and that one change kills most false positives.`],quote:`One dropped packet is weather. Three peers who can't reach you either — that's climate.`,table:{headers:[`Detector`,`Signal`,`False-positive risk`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Ping everyone, every round`,`Direct, fresh`,`Low, but O(N) traffic per node`,`Tiny clusters`],[`One direct ping, timeout = dead`,`Direct only`,`High — one blip kills a node`,`Never`],[`SWIM indirect probing`,`Multiple independent peers`,`Low — needs consensus to mark dead`,`Default choice`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Gossip Protocol`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-gossip.html`}},{heading:`04 · Phi accrual: 'dead' is a probability, not a bit`,body:[`SWIM gives you a boolean: alive or suspect. Cassandra looked at that and said, 'networks are messier than booleans.' Enter the phi accrual failure detector — instead of yes/no, it continuously outputs φ, essentially a score for how likely the node is dead, accrued from the history of heartbeat arrival times.`,`Why bother? Because 'no heartbeat for 3 seconds' means something very different on a LAN than over a transcontinental WAN link. Phi accrual lets the application set the threshold: crank it up and you tolerate flaky links but detect real failures slower; crank it down and you're fast but jumpy. The failure detector becomes a tunable dial instead of a hardcoded constant.`,`Operationally, this is also a gift for monitoring: φ is a number you can graph. Watch the curve climb before anyone crosses the threshold and you've got early warning instead of a post-mortem.`],quote:`'Dead' isn't a bit — it's a probability, and you get to choose how much doubt you can afford.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — Gossip Protocol`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-gossip.html`}},{heading:`05 · When gossip bites back: islands, seeds, and the ops tax`,body:[`Now let's talk about the part the papers wave through: the ops tax. Gossip assumes the epidemic can reach every node. If your seed lists don't overlap — or a firewall splits the gossip port — you don't get an error. You get two healthy clusters that each believe they're the whole truth. That's the 09:15 during the morning rush story I opened with, and it's the most expensive failure mode in this lesson precisely because nothing alarms.`,`So the defenses: keep at least one seed reachable from every rack and region; alert on cluster membership count, not per-node health — a count that drifts from N is the only signal islands give you; and watch gossip traffic, because it's constant background load even when nothing changes.`,`And the honest caveat to close on: if you're under about 50 nodes with stable membership, gossip is overkill. A central registry is easier to debug, easier to reason about, and the consistency model is one you can explain to the on-call. Reach for the epidemic when the coordinator becomes the problem — not before.`],quote:`Gossip's worst failure mode isn't an error — it's two confident, consistent, wrong halves.`,table:{headers:[`Situation`,`Move`],rows:[[`< 50 nodes, stable membership`,`Central registry (etcd, Consul KV)`],[`100s–1000s of nodes, churny membership`,`Gossip with SWIM detection`],[`Seed lists don't overlap`,`Stop and fix before scaling`],[`Membership count drifts from N`,`Page someone — islands are silent`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Gossip Protocol`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-gossip.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`epidemic-spread`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One rumor, three rounds: the doubling curve`,caption:`Each informed node tells one random peer per round — the informed set doubles until everyone knows in log₂(N) rounds.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph R0["Round 0 — 1 node knows"]
    A1["N1 ✓"]
    A2["N2 ·"]
    A3["N3 ·"]
    A4["N4 ·"]
  end
  subgraph R1["Round 1 — 2 know"]
    B1["N1 ✓"]
    B2["N2 ✓"]
    B3["N3 ·"]
    B4["N4 ·"]
  end
  subgraph R2["Round 2 — 4 know"]
    C1["N1 ✓"]
    C2["N2 ✓"]
    C3["N3 ✓"]
    C4["N4 ✓"]
  end
  A1 -- "tells" --> B2
  B1 -- "tells" --> C3
  B2 -- "tells" --> C4`},{id:`swim-probe`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`SWIM: no death sentence off a single ping`,caption:`A direct timeout triggers indirect probes through random peers; only independent confirmations promote a node to suspect.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant A as Node A (prober)
  participant B as Node B (suspect)
  participant C as Node C
  participant D as Node D
  A->>B: direct ping
  B--xA: no ack (timeout)
  A->>C: probe B for me
  A->>D: probe B for me
  C->>B: indirect ping
  D->>B: indirect ping
  C-->>A: cannot reach B
  D-->>A: cannot reach B
  Note over A: 2/2 independent confirmations → mark B suspect, gossip the status change`}],takeaways:[`Under ~50 nodes with stable membership, skip gossip — a central registry (etcd, Consul KV) is simpler to run and debug.`,`Budget log₂(N) rounds for a rumor to fully spread: 1,000 nodes ≈ 10 rounds, ~10 seconds at one gossip per second.`,`Gossip costs O(N log N) total messages per change and O(1) per node per round — a fanout of 1 peer keeps background traffic flat as you grow.`,`Never mark a node dead off one missed ping — require 2–3 independent indirect confirmations (SWIM) or every network blip becomes a false corpse.`,`Alert on cluster membership count, not node health, and keep seed lists overlapping — islands are silent, healthy-looking, and the most expensive gossip failure mode.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Gossip Protocol`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-gossip.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-hinted-handoff`,pathId:`distributed-systems`,order:4,title:`Hinted Handoff`,subtitle:`A node is down. A write needs to land somewhere. Hinted handoff: store the write on a healthy peer with a note saying "this belongs to node X"; when X recovers, the peer delivers. `,minutes:7,difficulty:3.5,tags:[`Cassandra`,`Dynamo`,`Riak`,`sloppy quorum`],story:{headline:`2:47 a.m., and the checkout page went red.`,paragraphs:[`Flash sale, 20× traffic, and at 2:47 a.m. node A-7 in the cart cluster dropped off the gossip ring — a flapping NIC we'd been meaning to replace for weeks. The error rate didn't spike everywhere; it crept up on a slice of carts. Every key whose replica set included A-7, hitting a neighbor that was also mid-GC-pause, failed its W=2 quorum and rejected. The support inbox filled with the same ticket: 'my cart keeps emptying itself.'`,`The maddening part: the cluster had dozens of healthy nodes with idle disk and IOPS to spare. It refused to use them because the quorum math only counted the natural replicas. We weren't out of capacity — we were out of imagination. The fix was to let a healthy neighbor sign for the package and leave a note: sloppy quorum plus hinted handoff. Writes accepted straight through the partition; when A-7 rejoined at 4:15 a.m., its peers drained hints and the cluster converged without a human in the loop.`,`What the night actually taught: keeping writes flowing during failure is a decision you make in the design doc, not at 3 a.m. And convergence afterwards is a second mechanism — hints plus repair — that you also have to design, throttle, and monitor before the pager goes off.`],problem:`How do you keep accepting writes while a replica is down — without pretending the down node is fine, and without losing track of what still needs to land there?`},tech:{stack:[`Dynamo`,`Cassandra`,`Riak`,`Sloppy Quorum`,`Hint Log`,`Merkle Repair`],note:`Hinted handoff is the canonical AP survival pattern: a healthy stand-in stores a downed replica's writes with a delivery note, drains them on recovery, and Merkle-tree anti-entropy catches whatever the hints drop.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why one dead node poisons a whole key range`,body:[`Here's the math that ruined our night. N=3, W=2. One node dead: writes to its keys still succeed — the two live replicas cover the quorum. But that dead node's copy is now silently falling behind, and any read that happens to touch it can serve yesterday's cart. Add one more problem — a long GC pause, a saturated disk, a flapping NIC — and W=2 can't be met at all. The write rejects. Not because the cluster lacked capacity, but because the quorum math only counts the natural replicas.`,`People ask: why not just retry? Retrying hits the same replica set — the topology didn't change, so the retry fails too. Why not drop W to 1? Now every write in the system has single-node durability, all the time, to handle a rare case. Why not add more replicas? You've just moved the arithmetic. The real question is different: can the write land on a healthy node that isn't a natural replica, with a note to deliver it home later? That's the door hinted handoff opens.`],quote:`The strict-quorum write that blocks is also the strict-quorum write that loses a sale.`,table:{headers:[`Failure scenario`,`Strict quorum (N=3, W=2)`,`Sloppy quorum + hints`],rows:[[`1 of 3 replicas down`,`Writes succeed; down node's copy falls behind`,`Writes succeed; stand-in holds the missing copy`],[`1 down + 1 slow/unreachable`,`W=2 unmet — write rejected`,`W met by any 2 reachable nodes`],[`2 of 3 down`,`Rejected for that key range`,`Writes land on stand-ins; durability thinned, availability kept`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Hinted Handoff`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-hinted-handoff.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the write that signs for its neighbor's package`,body:[`Walk the ring with me. Key K's natural replicas are {A, B, C}; A is down. The coordinator writes to B and C as usual, then — instead of failing A's copy — it walks the preference list to the next healthy node, D, and writes there with a hint attached: 'this row belongs to A.' Sloppy quorum means W counts reachable nodes, not specific ones, so {B, C, D} satisfies W=2 and the client gets its ack.`,`The hint itself is tiny metadata on D — Cassandra literally keeps per-destination hint files. D probes A on a cadence; the moment A answers, D replays its hint log, A applies the writes as if they'd arrived normally, and D deletes what it delivered. One honest trade to say out loud: sloppy quorum weakens the strict read-write overlap guarantee. Dynamo runs sloppy reads too, so read-your-writes holds inside the sloppy set; Cassandra keeps reads on natural replicas and treats hints as background catch-up. Know which contract your database signed.`],quote:`A quorum is a promise about which nodes; a sloppy quorum is a promise about how many.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — Hinted Handoff`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-hinted-handoff.html`}},{heading:`03 · The recovery loop — and why hints are a fast path, not a guarantee`,body:[`The recovery loop sounds gentle until you size it. A node down for twelve hours leaves its neighbors holding millions of hints. If they replay at full speed the moment it rejoins, you've built a self-inflicted outage: memtable pressure, compaction backlog, dropped mutations — on the node that's already the weakest kid in the class. Throttle the drain to a fraction of normal write throughput and let it take an hour to catch up.`,`And here's the part people skip: hints are best-effort. D can crash holding an undelivered log. Hints expire after a configurable window — Cassandra's max_hint_window, historically three hours, now days by default — and anything past the window is gone from the fast path. That's why anti-entropy exists: replicas compare Merkle trees per token range, find the divergent ranges, and stream just those rows. Hints are the fast path; repair is the guarantee. Run both, or you've built a system that converges only when it feels like it.`],quote:`Hints are a promise to remember, not a guarantee to deliver — anti-entropy is the guarantee.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — Hinted Handoff`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-hinted-handoff.html`}},{heading:`04 · Choosing your posture: where hints save you, and where they lie`,body:[`So where do you turn this on? Carts, sessions, wishlists, activity feeds — anywhere brief staleness beats a blocked user. Turn it on, tune the throttle and the window, and sleep through node failures. Payments, ledgers, inventory decrements — the opposite call. A rejected write with a clear error is recoverable; a silently thinned durability guarantee is not. Dynamo and Riak bake hinted handoff into the core; Cassandra gives you hinted_handoff_enabled plus throttle and window knobs — set them per workload, not per fashion.`,`And instrument the thing. Hint queue depth, oldest-hint age, drain rate — those three graphs tell you whether the next partition will converge politely or silently drop writes. When oldest-hint age starts creeping toward your hint window, the system is telling you, in advance, exactly how it will fail. Listen.`],quote:`Availability is a per-data-type decision, not a cluster-wide flag.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Strict quorum`,`Strong overlap guarantee; clear errors`,`Blocks when natural replicas unreachable`,`Payments, ledgers, inventory`],[`Sloppy quorum + hinted handoff`,`Accepts writes through partitions`,`Brief staleness; hint storms; expiry`,`Carts, sessions, feeds`],[`Client-side retry buffer`,`No database changes needed`,`Blind to topology; retry storms`,`Thin clients, low volume`],[`Anti-entropy only`,`Simple; always converges eventually`,`Long divergence windows; stale reads`,`Backstop — never the only plan`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Hinted Handoff`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-hinted-handoff.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`write-path-hints`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Write path with hinted handoff`,caption:`The coordinator walks past the dead replica to a healthy stand-in; the hint rides along with the data until A comes home.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    CLI["Client: PUT cart:42"] --> CO["Coordinator"]
    A["Node A — natural replica, DOWN"] -. unreachable .-> CO
    CO -->|"replica write"| B["Node B — natural replica"]
    CO -->|"replica write"| C["Node C — natural replica"]
    CO -->|"write + hint: deliver to A"| D["Node D — stand-in, hint log"]
    D ==>|"A recovers: replay hints, throttled"| A
    classDef down fill:#ffdddd,stroke:#cc0000,color:#7a0000
    classDef hint fill:#fff3d6,stroke:#b8860b
    class A down
    class D hint`},{id:`drain-and-repair`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Hint drain and the anti-entropy backstop`,caption:`Once A returns, D replays its hint log throttled and deletes on ack; Merkle-tree repair catches anything the hints lost.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant D as Node D — hint holder
    participant A as Node A — was down
    participant B as Node B — healthy peer
    loop every ~60s until hint log empty
        D->>A: gossip heartbeat — back up?
        A-->>D: alive
        D->>A: replay hints, throttled, oldest first
        A-->>D: applied
        D->>D: delete delivered hints
    end
    Note over D,A: Hints can be lost — D crashed, or hint TTL expired
    Note over A,B: Periodic anti-entropy backstop
    A->>B: exchange Merkle tree digests per token range
    B-->>A: mismatch — stream divergent keys
    A->>A: reconcile and converge`}],takeaways:[`Budget hint disk on stand-ins: outage_hours × 3600 × writes_per_sec ≈ queued writes — a node down 4h at 2k writes/s queues ~29M writes on its neighbors.`,`Set hint TTL at least 2× your worst realistic node recovery time; a 3-hour hint window silently drops every write from a longer outage.`,`Throttle hint replay to ≤10–20% of the returning node's normal write throughput, or the rejoin becomes its own incident.`,`Split posture by data class: carts/sessions → hinted handoff ON; payments/ledgers → strict quorum on natural replicas with a visible error.`,`Run anti-entropy repair on a cadence shorter than hint expiry (e.g., weekly repair against a 10-day window) and page when oldest-hint age crosses 50% of TTL.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Hinted Handoff`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-hinted-handoff.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-high-availability`,pathId:`distributed-systems`,order:5,title:`High Availability`,subtitle:`"99.99% uptime" sounds great until you compute that's 53 minutes of downtime per year. SLAs, SLOs, error budgets — the vocabulary of availability is precise; the engineering follow`,minutes:10,difficulty:3.5,tags:[`SLA`,`SLI`,`SLO`,`error budget`],story:{headline:`Forty-seven minutes, or: how we spent a year of error budget in one night`,paragraphs:[`We closed the deal on a Thursday. 99.99% availability, written into the contract, because the customer asked and nobody wanted to be the engineer who lost the deal by doing math out loud. 99.99% is 53 minutes of downtime a year. Hold that number — because the following Tuesday at 14:30 right after a canary deploy, we spent 47 of those minutes in one shot.`,`It started with a 30-second DNS blip at our payment provider. Annoying, not fatal — except every in-flight request retried three times with no backoff and no jitter, so a 30-second blip became a 3× request flood. Thread pools filled, health checks started failing, the load balancer began bouncing instances, and the circuit breaker — which existed, which was configured — had its threshold set so high it never opened. 'Payments are degraded' became 'the whole product is down' in about four minutes.`,`The worst part wasn't the outage. It was the error budget dashboard the next morning: a quarter's budget, gone in a single night, on an incident that should have been a footnote. That's the night I stopped treating availability as a badge and started treating it as a budget — a finite pile of minutes you either spend on purpose or lose by accident.`],problem:`How do you pick an availability target you can actually engineer and keep — and how do you stop one bad night from spending a year of it?`},tech:{stack:[`SLI`,`SLO`,`Error Budget`,`Multi-AZ`,`Circuit Breaker`,`Retry + Jitter`,`Graceful Degradation`],note:`What you measure, what you promise, and the machinery that buys back nines: a budget to spend, breakers to trip, and fallbacks to fall to.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: what does a nine actually cost?`,body:[`Let's do the math out loud before anyone falls in love with a number. 99% availability sounds fine until you realize it's 3.65 days of downtime a year — your product simply gone for a long weekend, annually. 99.9% is 8.76 hours. 99.99% is 52.6 minutes. 99.999% is 5.26 minutes. And each nine is roughly 10× the engineering: 99 to 99.9 is a few well-placed retries and real monitoring; 99.9 to 99.99 is multi-AZ; 99.99 to 99.999 is multi-region, careful operations, and chaos engineering.`,`Here's the trap: the cost curve is exponential, but the value curve usually isn't. Nobody churns because your internal admin tool was down for an hour — so why not always pick 99%? Because the flip side is real too: for a checkout flow, an hour down at peak is revenue and trust you don't get back. The target isn't a badge, it's a bet — and you should be able to say, in minutes, exactly what you're buying.`],quote:`The nines lie. The downtime budget tells the truth.`,table:{headers:[`SLO`,`Downtime / year`,`Downtime / month`,`Downtime / week`],rows:[[`99%`,`3.65 days`,`7.3 hours`,`1.68 hours`],[`99.9%`,`8.76 hours`,`43.8 min`,`10.1 min`],[`99.95%`,`4.38 hours`,`21.9 min`,`5.04 min`],[`99.99%`,`52.6 min`,`4.38 min`,`1.01 min`],[`99.999%`,`5.26 min`,`26 sec`,`6.05 sec`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — High Availability`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-high-availability.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: SLI, SLO, SLA, budget`,body:[`Four words get thrown around interchangeably, and they are not interchangeable. SLI — Service Level Indicator — is what you measure: the fraction of requests returning 2xx, p99 latency under 300ms, the fraction of writes that durably persist. It's a number computed from telemetry, full stop. SLO — the objective — is the internal commitment: 'that indicator stays ≥ 99.9% over a 30-day rolling window.' SLA — the agreement — is the contract with customers, usually with refunds attached.`,`The ordering rule that saves careers: your SLO must be tighter than your SLA. If the contract says 99.95%, your internal target should be 99.99% — you want to hit 'internally unacceptable' long before you hit 'we owe money.' And the error budget is just 1 − SLO: at 99.9%, that's 0.1%, about 43 minutes a month of officially-OK-to-be-down. Why not skip all this vocabulary and just 'be reliable'? Because without a number, every outage is a vibe — and vibes don't freeze feature launches.`],quote:`An SLA without a tighter SLO behind it is a promise funded by someone else's pager.`,table:{headers:[`Term`,`What it is`,`Who it's for`,`Example`],rows:[[`SLI`,`The measurement`,`Engineers`,`Fraction of 2xx responses`],[`SLO`,`The internal target`,`The team`,`SLI ≥ 99.9% over 30 days`],[`SLA`,`The contract`,`Customers`,`< 99.95% triggers refunds`],[`Error budget`,`1 − SLO`,`Everyone`,`43 min/month at 99.9%`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — High Availability`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-high-availability.html`}},{heading:`03 · Why not just add more nines? Composability bites`,body:[`Now the part nobody puts on the slide: availability multiplies, it never adds. If your service leans on five dependencies, each at a lovely 99.9%, your aggregate ceiling is 0.999^5 ≈ 99.5% — half a nine worse than your worst dependency, before you've written a single line of your own code. Ten dependencies? 99.0%. Twenty? You're at 98% while your marketing page still says four nines.`,`So why not just make every dependency five nines? Because that's the exponential cost curve again, now applied to someone else's roadmap. The realistic moves are claw-backs: cache what you can serve stale, retry what's transient, degrade what's optional. And be honest about the dependency nobody lists — your own deploys. Most outages are self-inflicted, which is exactly why the error budget exists: it's the line item for breaking things on purpose, safely.`],quote:`Availability multiplies; it never adds.`,table:{headers:[`Dependencies at 99.9%`,`Your ceiling`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`1`,`99.90%`,`You're only as good as it`],[`5`,`99.50%`,`Half a nine gone`],[`10`,`99.00%`,`Marketing page is now fiction`],[`20`,`98.02%`,`Redesign the dependency graph`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — High Availability`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-high-availability.html`}},{heading:`04 · The patterns that buy 9s — and their price tags`,body:[`So which patterns actually buy nines? Ranked by return on pain: the cheapest nine you'll ever get is graceful degradation — don't let one feature's failure take down the product. Recommendations down? Show a static list. Search down? Show recent items. Most outages in the bad sense are full outages; partial degradation barely registers on a user's Tuesday.`,`Above that: retries with exponential backoff and jitter recover most transient errors for the price of a few lines; circuit breakers stop one sick dependency from becoming a cascade; multi-AZ takes you from ~99.95 to ~99.99 at the cost of 2–3× replicas; multi-region active-active pushes toward 99.999 but now you own conflict resolution. Chaos engineering is how you find out — in daylight, on your terms — whether any of it actually works. Why not buy all of it? Because every pattern has a standing cost, and idle reliability is the most expensive feature you never shipped.`],quote:`The cheapest nine is the one you get by failing gracefully.`,table:{headers:[`Pattern`,`Buys you`,`Cost`],rows:[[`Multi-AZ`,`~99.95 → 99.99`,`2–3× storage / replicas`],[`Multi-region active/passive`,`~99.99 → 99.995`,`~2× total infra`],[`Multi-region active/active`,`~99.995 → 99.999`,`3× total + conflict resolution`],[`Retries with backoff`,`Recovers transient errors`,`None (just code)`],[`Circuit breakers`,`Stops cascade failures`,`Library config`],[`Graceful degradation`,`Partial instead of full outage`,`Feature flags, fallback paths`],[`Chaos engineering`,`Finds bugs before customers`,`Engineering investment`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — High Availability`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-high-availability.html`}},{heading:`05 · Spending the budget like an owner`,body:[`Last thing, and it's the one that makes the other four stick: a budget without a policy is just a chart. Write the policy down. Budget healthy — more than half left — ship features normally. Budget low — under a quarter — reliability work only. Budget spent — feature freeze, and the freeze lifts when the SLO recovers, not when someone senior gets annoyed.`,`And the counterintuitive bit: if you never breach your SLO, it's too loose — you're paying for reliability your users don't need, and you should spend some of that budget on faster deploys or riskier experiments. Our 3 AM taught us the other direction: 47 minutes against a 52.6-minute annual budget isn't an outage, it's a spending problem. Spend on purpose, or lose by accident.`],quote:`An error budget you never spend is an SLO you set too loose.`,table:{headers:[`Budget state`,`Policy`],rows:[[`> 50% remaining`,`Ship features normally; spend on deploys`],[`25–50%`,`Watch it; prioritize reliability debt`],[`< 25%`,`Reliability work only`],[`Spent (0%)`,`Feature freeze until SLO recovers`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`outage-cascade`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The 3 AM cascade vs. the same blip today`,caption:`Left: how a 30-second dependency blip became a 47-minute full outage. Right: the same blip with backoff, jitter, a real breaker threshold, and a fallback path.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
  subgraph NIGHT["3 AM — what happened"]
    A["Payment provider DNS blip<br/>~30s of timeouts"] --> B["Every request retries 3x<br/>no backoff, no jitter"]
    B --> C["Effective load ×3<br/>thread pools fill"]
    C --> D["Health checks fail<br/>LB bounces instances"]
    D --> E["Breaker threshold too high<br/>never opens"]
    E --> F["Full product outage<br/>47 minutes"]
  end
  subgraph NOW["Today — same blip"]
    G["Dependency timeouts"] --> H["Retry max 2<br/>backoff + jitter"]
    H --> I["Breaker opens<br/>5 failures in 10s"]
    I --> J["Fallback: cached quotes<br/>charges queued"]
    J --> K["Checkout degraded<br/>product stays up"]
  end`},{id:`breaker-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Request path through the breaker and fallback`,caption:`The sequence that keeps a dependency blip from becoming an outage: bounded retries, fail-fast when the breaker opens, degraded-but-alive responses from the fallback cache.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant U as Client
  participant S as Service
  participant CB as Circuit Breaker
  participant D as Dependency
  participant F as Fallback Cache
  U->>S: checkout request
  S->>CB: call payment provider
  CB->>D: attempt 1
  D-->>CB: timeout
  Note over CB: retry with backoff + jitter
  CB->>D: attempt 2
  D-->>CB: timeout
  Note over CB: 5 failures in 10s — breaker opens
  CB-->>S: fail fast, no network call
  S->>F: read cached quote, queue the charge
  F-->>S: stale-but-fine response
  S-->>U: 200 OK, degraded mode
  Note over CB,D: after 30s, half-open probe lets one request through`}],takeaways:[`Do the math before signing: 99.9% = 8.76 h/yr, 99.99% = 52.6 min/yr, 99.999% = 5.26 min/yr. If you can't name what fills that budget, don't promise it.`,`Five dependencies at 99.9% make you 99.5% (0.999^5 ≈ 99.5%). Count your dependencies before you count your nines.`,`Set the internal SLO roughly 10× tighter than the SLA — e.g. 99.99% internal against a 99.9% contract — so you breach internally before you owe refunds.`,`Cap retries at 2–3 with exponential backoff plus jitter; naive unlimited retries turn a 30-second dependency blip into a 3× load spike.`,`Write the error budget policy down: at 99.9% you get ~43 min/month — spend it on deploys, and freeze features when it hits zero.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — High Availability`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-high-availability.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-cell-architecture`,pathId:`distributed-systems`,order:6,title:`Cell-based Architecture`,subtitle:`Cell-based architecture partitions a multi-tenant platform into independent, identical units called cells. A cell handles a slice of customers end-to-end. When one cell fails, the `,minutes:12,difficulty:4.5,tags:[`AWS cells`,`Shopify pods`,`cell router`],story:{headline:`11:20 during end-of-month billing: one tenant's export job turned the status page red for every customer we had.`,paragraphs:[`The pager went off at 16:45 on a pre-release Friday. Postgres CPU pegged at 100%, the connection pool exhausted, dashboards spinning for every customer on the platform. The culprit, buried in pg_stat_activity: a single tenant had kicked off a year-end export — a batch job hammering the one database we all shared. No deploy had gone out. No bug had shipped. One customer's bad night was now four thousand customers' bad night, and the status page was a wall of red.`,`The postmortem the next morning was uncomfortable precisely because there was nothing to fix. Rate-limit the tenant? Fine — until a migration locks a hot table, or the Redis cluster fails over, or a bad deploy lands. We didn't have a bug problem; we had a shared-fate problem. One big database, one big cache, one worker pool meant the blast radius of anything was everyone.`,`The fix wasn't more capacity or smarter queries. It was drawing a line around each slice of customers and giving it its own everything — its own Postgres, its own Redis, its own workers — with one boring router in front deciding who goes where. Cells. The pattern AWS, Shopify, and Slack all converged on independently, and the most underrated SaaS architecture of the last decade.`],problem:`How do you partition a multi-tenant platform so that no single tenant, deploy, or infrastructure failure can take down more than 1/N of your customers?`},tech:{stack:[`Cell Router`,`DNS Routing`,`JWT Cell Claims`,`Per-Cell Postgres`,`Per-Cell Redis`,`Wave Deploys`,`Tenant Migration`],note:`One boring shared router in front of N identical full-stack cells — each cell owns its database, cache, broker, and workers, and shares nothing in the hot path.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why does one tenant take everyone down?`,body:[`Let's start with the 10:00 at peak checkout volume picture. One big Postgres, one big Redis, one worker pool, thousands of tenants stacked on top. A tenant fires a batch job, a hot lock shows up, the cache fails over — and every one of those events lands on every customer at once. That's not bad luck; that's the design. In a shared system, the blast radius of any failure is the entire customer base by construction.`,`Now, the obvious pushback: 'just add rate limiting and noisy-neighbor controls.' Sure — and you should. But rate limiting doesn't save you from a bad migration, a bad deploy, or a cache cluster dying. Those are platform-level failures, and as long as the platform is one thing, platform-level failures are customer-level outages. You can't patch your way out of shared fate; you have to partition it away.`,`That's the whole bet of cells: N small identical systems with disjoint customer sets, instead of one big shared one. When a cell fails, 1/N of customers have a bad day — and everyone else never notices.`],quote:`In a shared system, every customer is one tenant's bad day away from a Sev-2.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`One shared system`,`Simple ops, one deployment, easy cross-tenant queries`,`Blast radius = everyone; noisy neighbors; one bad deploy hits all`,`< ~1k tenants, low stakes`],[`Cell-based`,`Blast radius = 1/N; independent failures and deploys per cell`,`More databases to operate; router is a new seam; migration tooling needed`,`> 1k tenants, B2B SaaS`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Cell-based Architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-cell-architecture.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what's actually inside a cell?`,body:[`Here's the part people get wrong: a cell is not a microservice, and it's not a region. It's a full vertical slice — API tier, database, cache, message broker, background workers — for one slice of customers. The application code is identical in every cell; the cell is a deployment unit, not a different product. You ship the same feature to every cell, just in waves: canary first, then 10% of cells, then everything. A schema migration runs in every cell's database, one at a time.`,`So how big is a cell? This is the sizing decision that makes or breaks the pattern. Too small and you're operating hundreds of tiny databases and drowning in toil. Too big and your 'blast radius' quietly becomes 'platform-wide' again. The sweet spot — and what AWS's published guidance says — is a cell small enough that a human can reason about it during an incident, large enough that you don't end up with a thousand of them. In practice: roughly 5–10k tenants per cell, a few thousand RPS.`],quote:`A cell isn't a microservice — it's the whole product, shrunk to fit one slice of customers.`,table:{headers:[`Cell Size`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Too small (hundreds of tenants)`,`Tiny blast radius; cheap tenant migrations`,`Operational toil explodes; hundreds of DBs to run`,`Rarely right`],[`Sweet spot (~5–10k tenants)`,`Human-reasonable in incidents; manageable cell count`,`Needs real sizing discipline`,`Most B2B SaaS`],[`Too big (platform-scale)`,`Few cells to operate`,`Blast radius creeps back to 'everyone'`,`Defeats the purpose`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Cell-based Architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-cell-architecture.html`}},{heading:`03 · The router is the only seam — keep it boring`,body:[`Every cell architecture has exactly one shared component: the router. It answers one question — which cell does this tenant live in? — and forwards the request. That's it. The moment you give the router a hot-path database call, or a feature-flag lookup, or anything clever, you've rebuilt the single point of failure the cells existed to remove. Keep it a small, highly-available service with a tiny mapping table cached aggressively.`,`Where does the mapping live? Three options, in order of operational simplicity. DNS: each tenant gets {tenant}.api.example.com resolving straight to a cell IP — zero hot-path lookup, and the DNS TTL is your propagation knob when you move tenants. Path-based: /{tenant}/... and the router parses the URL. Or JWT: the user's auth token carries the cell ID, and the router validates and routes. DNS-based is the most popular for a reason — it pushes the routing decision onto infrastructure that's been battle-tested for thirty years.`],quote:`If your router calls a database in the hot path, you just rebuilt the monolith with extra steps.`,table:{headers:[`Mapping Option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`DNS ({tenant}.api.example.com)`,`Zero hot-path lookup; TTL is the migration knob`,`Propagation lag on moves; wildcard certs per cell`,`Most popular default`],[`Path-based (/{tenant}/...)`,`No DNS changes; easy to inspect and debug`,`Router parses every request; tenant in every URL`,`Simple setups`],[`JWT cell claim`,`Stateless; cryptographically bound to the user`,`Stale until token refresh; coupled to auth issuance`,`Auth-heavy platforms`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Cell-based Architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-cell-architecture.html`}},{heading:`04 · Tenants move — build the moving truck on day one`,body:[`Here's the operational truth nobody puts in the architecture diagram: tenants grow at different rates. Six months in, cell 2 is hot and cell 5 is idle, and now you need to move tenants between cells. The procedure itself is simple — snapshot the tenant's data in cell A, replay it onto cell B, flip the router mapping, verify, clean up cell A. The hard part is treating that as a product, not a project.`,`If 'move a tenant' is a two-week engineering epic, you will never rebalance, and your cells will drift until the pattern rots. Automate the move, run it routinely, make it boring. Shopify moves merchants between pods all the time — that's the bar. The router mapping plus the DNS TTL is what turns the switchover into a non-event instead of a maintenance window.`],quote:`A cell boundary you can't move tenants across isn't a seam — it's a wall.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — Cell-based Architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-cell-architecture.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`cell-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Cell topology — one boring router, N identical cells`,caption:`The only shared component is the router; every cell is a full vertical slice with its own database, cache, broker, and workers.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  R["Cell Router<br/>tenant → cell map<br/>cached, boring"]
  T1["Tenant A"] --> R
  T2["Tenant B"] --> R
  T3["Tenant C"] --> R
  subgraph C1["Cell 1 · ~5–10k tenants"]
    A1["API tier"] --> A2["Postgres"]
    A1 --> A3["Redis"]
    A1 --> A4["Broker"]
    A4 --> A5["Workers"]
  end
  subgraph C2["Cell 2 · ~5–10k tenants"]
    B1["API tier"] --> B2["Postgres"]
    B1 --> B3["Redis"]
    B1 --> B4["Broker"]
    B4 --> B5["Workers"]
  end
  R --> A1
  R --> B1`},{id:`tenant-migration`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Moving a tenant from Cell A to Cell B`,caption:`Snapshot → replay → flip → verify → clean up — automated and routine, not a one-off engineering project.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant Ops as Migration tool
  participant A as Cell A
  participant B as Cell B
  participant R as Cell Router
  Ops->>A: Snapshot tenant data
  Ops->>B: Replay snapshot
  Ops->>B: Verify data + smoke tests
  Ops->>R: Flip mapping — tenant routes to Cell B
  R-->>Ops: Routing updated (DNS TTL / map push)
  Ops->>A: Clean up tenant in Cell A
  Note over A,B: Same code, two homes — the tenant never notices`}],takeaways:[`Multi-tenant B2B SaaS with >1,000 tenants: make cells the default — size each cell for ~5–10k tenants and a few thousand RPS.`,`Share nothing in the hot path: per-cell database, cache, and broker; the router is the only shared layer and must make zero hot-path database calls.`,`Route via DNS ({tenant}.api.example.com) or a JWT cell claim; keep the mapping table tiny and cached aggressively, with DNS TTL as your propagation knob.`,`Deploy in waves — canary → 10% of cells → all cells — and run schema migrations one cell at a time, so a bad release touches one cell, not the platform.`,`Ship tenant-migration tooling on day one and run it routinely (Shopify moves merchants between pods constantly); if a move is a two-week project, your cells will drift and the pattern rots.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Cell-based Architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-cell-architecture.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-cybersecurity-terms`,pathId:`distributed-systems`,order:7,title:`Cybersecurity Terms`,subtitle:`Software engineers regularly read security tickets, threat models, and audit reports. The vocabulary is dense; the categories are real; the failure modes are recurrent. Twenty term`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[],story:{headline:`The 15:10 during a database replica failover login storm that forced me to learn the words`,paragraphs:[`Tuesday, 08:45 as mobile traffic surged. PagerDuty fires: auth failure rate up 40x. The dashboard shows 38,000 login attempts against /login in ten minutes, from roughly 2,000 residential IPs — and the part that made my stomach drop: 214 successful logins. Real accounts. Accounts I now had to assume belonged to someone else.`,`My first Slack message was 'auth is broken??' — and that phrase was useless, because it wasn't. Authentication worked flawlessly: right password, right user, welcome aboard. The users had reused passwords from some unrelated breach dump, and someone was replaying them at us at industrial scale. Credential stuffing. The system did exactly what it was told; what it was told was dumb. That distinction — AuthN fine, AuthZ fine, credential hygiene catastrophic — is only visible if you speak the vocabulary.`,`The postmortem became a forced glossary: credential stuffing (the attack), MFA and passkeys (the fix), rate limiting on auth endpoints, an HMAC check we discovered missing on webhooks during the review, an SSRF in our internal fetcher the pen-test flagged two weeks later. Every finding arrived as a term I had to decode under pressure. This lesson is the glossary I wish I'd had at 13:20 right after the schema migration.`],problem:`Security findings arrive as dense vocabulary. Without the glossary you can't triage, can't scope, and can't tell a P0 from a nitpick — so you either panic or dismiss, and both are failures.`},tech:{stack:[`OAuth 2.0 + OIDC`,`JWT`,`WebAuthn Passkeys`,`Argon2id`,`HMAC Signatures`,`mTLS`,`RBAC Policies`,`CSP Headers`,`WAF`,`KMS / HSM`],note:`The identity, crypto, and control-plane pieces every security finding touches — from proving who someone is, to proving a message is intact, to the headers and policies that blunt the top ten attacks.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: 'is auth broken?' is the wrong question`,body:[`Here's what I got wrong at 17:00 as cross-region traffic shifted: I typed 'auth broken??' before I'd looked at a single log line. That phrase is useless because 'auth' is two completely different questions wearing one trench coat. Authentication — who are you? Authorisation — what are you allowed to do? Our authentication was flawless. The users had reused passwords from a breach elsewhere, and an attacker was replaying them at scale. The system did exactly what it was told; what it was told was dumb.`,`Once you split AuthN from AuthZ, the whole identity zoo snaps into place. SSO means you authenticate once at an identity provider and everything downstream trusts that token. OAuth 2.0 is delegated authorisation — 'app, you may act on my behalf at Service X' — and OIDC bolts the identity layer on top, which is why it's the default for user login in 2026. SAML is the XML-era ancestor still haunting enterprise SSO. MFA adds a second factor; passkeys go further and delete the password entirely. RBAC vs ABAC is just: do your permissions fit in a handful of roles, or do you need policy expressions? Most teams need RBAC and reach for ABAC because it sounds smarter.`,`Zero Trust is the umbrella claim: never trust a packet because of where it came from. Network position is not identity. Every request re-proves itself. That sounds expensive until you realise you already do it at the API layer — you just weren't doing it service-to-service, and that's exactly where lateral movement lives.`],quote:`Authentication answers 'who are you?' Authorisation answers 'so what?' Most breaches are one answered correctly and the other never asked.`,table:{headers:[`Term`,`One-liner`,`Where it bites`],rows:[[`Authentication (AuthN)`,`Who are you? Password, MFA, passkey, cert.`,`Stuffing exploits reused passwords, not your code`],[`Authorisation (AuthZ)`,`What may you do? Roles, scopes, policies.`,`IDOR: valid user, wrong object`],[`SSO`,`Sign in once at an IdP; token vouches for many apps.`,`IdP outage = everything is down`],[`OAuth 2.0`,`Delegated authorisation: act on a user's behalf.`,`Confused-deputy if scopes are too broad`],[`OIDC`,`OAuth 2.0 + identity layer; the login standard.`,`Skip aud/exp validation = token confusion`],[`SAML`,`XML-based SSO; still common in enterprise.`,`Signature-wrapping vulnerabilities`],[`MFA / 2FA`,`Something you have + know + are.`,`SMS factors fall to SIM swaps`],[`Passkey / WebAuthn`,`Public-key auth, origin-bound; post-password.`,`Enrollment and account-recovery flows`],[`RBAC vs ABAC`,`Roles vs attribute-policy expressions.`,`ABAC sprawl becomes unauditable`],[`Zero Trust`,`Never trust, always verify; per-request authz.`,`Cost shows up in latency and ops maturity`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Cybersecurity Terms`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-cybersecurity-terms.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the crypto boundary`,body:[`Crypto vocabulary trips people because four words sound interchangeable and are not. A hash is a one-way shredder: same input, same fixed-length digest, no key, no reverse. Great for fingerprints and integrity, useless for secrecy. An HMAC is a hash with a secret stirred in — now the digest also proves the sender knows the secret. That's why every webhook you'll ever verify is HMAC-signed: it's not encryption, it's a wax seal. And a KDF — argon2id, bcrypt — is a hash deliberately slowed down and made memory-hungry, because its adversary is a GPU farm, not a corrupted packet.`,`The classic mistake: 'we hash passwords with SHA-256.' So did the breach dumps. SHA-256 does billions of guesses per second per core; argon2id is tuned so a single guess costs real memory and milliseconds. The entire point of a KDF is to make the attacker's math expensive while your login stays under ~100ms.`,`Then the asymmetric side: key pairs, certificates, PKI — the machinery behind HTTPS and mTLS. And HSM/KMS is the operational rule that matters most: keys never leave the secure store; signing happens inside. If your private key lives in an env var, you don't have key management, you have a countdown. JWTs ride on top of all this — a signed JSON payload of claims — and they're only as trustworthy as the signature check you actually perform.`],quote:`A hash proves the data didn't change; an HMAC proves who sent it; a KDF proves you weren't in a hurry.`,table:{headers:[`Primitive`,`Use it for`,`Never use it for`],rows:[[`SHA-256`,`Integrity, fingerprints, Merkle trees`,`Password storage — far too fast`],[`HMAC`,`Webhook signatures, API request signing`,`Hiding content — it's not encryption`],[`Argon2id / bcrypt`,`Password and key derivation`,`Anything latency-critical at high QPS`],[`RSA / ECDSA`,`Signatures, certificates, JWT signing`,`Bulk data encryption`],[`AES-GCM`,`Encrypting data at rest / in transit`,`Hand-rolled modes — use a library`],[`JWT`,`Signed claims between parties`,`Revocable sessions without a denylist`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Cybersecurity Terms`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-cybersecurity-terms.html`}},{heading:`03 · The attack catalogue is a checklist in disguise`,body:[`The attack catalogue looks like trivia until you notice it's a checklist written by everyone else's postmortems. SQL injection is user input concatenated into a query — the fix is parameterised queries, always, no exceptions, and yet it still tops the lists because one intern-written report builder reappears every year. XSS is attacker JS running in another user's browser — escape output, add a CSP header. CSRF is the victim's own authenticated browser being puppeteered — SameSite cookies and CSRF tokens. Each name is a failure mode with a known antidote.`,`The sneaky ones are the server-side sins. SSRF: your server is tricked into requesting internal URLs — the cloud metadata endpoint at 169.254.169.254 is the jackpot, so allow-list outbound and restrict IMDS access. RCE is game over — input validation, sandboxing, least privilege. Supply-chain: the dependency you never audited is the backdoor you never saw — SBOMs, pinned versions, signature verification. And credential stuffing, our 3 AM guest, is defeated not by cleverness but by MFA, passkeys, and rate limits on auth.`],quote:`Every attack name is a postmortem somebody else already wrote for you — read the glossary, skip the outage.`,table:{headers:[`Attack`,`What it is`,`Defence`],rows:[[`SQL injection`,`User input concatenated into a SQL query`,`Parameterised queries, always`],[`XSS`,`Attacker JS runs in another user's browser`,`Escape output; CSP header`],[`CSRF`,`Authenticated browser tricked into a request`,`SameSite cookies; CSRF tokens`],[`Phishing`,`User authenticates to the attacker`,`Passkeys / WebAuthn (origin-bound)`],[`Credential stuffing`,`Replay leaked passwords on your site`,`MFA; passkeys; rate limit on auth`],[`SSRF`,`Server tricked into requesting internal URLs`,`Allow-list outbound; restrict IMDS`],[`RCE`,`Attacker runs code on your server`,`Input validation; sandboxing; least privilege`],[`Supply-chain`,`Compromised dependency`,`SBOM; pin versions; signature verification`],[`DDoS`,`Overwhelm with traffic`,`CDN; WAF; rate limit; capacity headroom`],[`Man-in-the-middle`,`Eavesdropping / tampering on the wire`,`HTTPS / mTLS; cert pinning for clients`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Cybersecurity Terms`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-cybersecurity-terms.html`}},{heading:`04 · Why not just buy a WAF and move on?`,body:[`After the incident, someone in the budget meeting asked: 'can't we just buy a WAF and be done?' You can buy a WAF. It will catch the loud, sloppy stuff — the sqlmap scans, the obvious payloads. It will not catch the report builder that concatenates a customer ID into SQL, because that request looks byte-for-byte identical to a legitimate one. A WAF is pattern matching at the perimeter; injection lives in the semantics of your own code.`,`That's the whole argument for defence in depth: every layer assumes the previous one failed. Parameterised queries assume the WAF missed it. Least privilege assumes the query got through — the compromised service token can read one table, not drop the database. Zero Trust assumes the network is already hostile, so service-to-service calls carry mTLS and per-request authorisation. No layer is strong alone; together they turn one bug into a non-event.`,`And the honest trade-off: every control has friction. MFA adds a step; passkeys need enrollment flows; rate limits catch real users during shared-IP spikes. The art is spending friction where the attack frequency actually is — auth endpoints and admin surfaces — not sprinkling it everywhere.`],quote:`A WAF is a seatbelt made of regular expressions; the database still believes everything you tell it.`,table:{headers:[`Layer`,`Stops`,`Misses`],rows:[[`WAF`,`Scanners, known payloads, volumetric noise`,`Semantically valid malicious requests`],[`Parameterised queries`,`Injection at the data layer`,`AuthZ bugs — right query, wrong object`],[`Least privilege (IAM)`,`Blast radius of a stolen token`,`Initial compromise itself`],[`mTLS / Zero Trust`,`Lateral movement, spoofed services`,`Compromised legitimate credentials`],[`CSP headers`,`XSS payload execution`,`Server-side injection classes`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Cybersecurity Terms`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-cybersecurity-terms.html`}},{heading:`05 · The payoff: phishing-resistant by default`,body:[`Here's the term I'd tattoo on every auth codebase after that night: phishing-resistant. Not all factors are equal. A password is something you know — and once known, it's replayable forever, which is exactly what credential stuffing automates. SMS OTP is something you have, sort of — but SIM swaps and phishing proxies relay it in real time. TOTP apps are better; still phishable, because a user under pressure will type the code into the attacker's page.`,`Passkeys — WebAuthn — are different in kind, not degree. The private key never leaves the device, and the signature is bound to the origin, so a lookalike domain literally cannot obtain a valid assertion. Phishing a passkey isn't 'harder' — it's protocol-breaking. That's why every major IdP is pushing them: it deletes the password-replay class of attack, which is precisely what hit us at 12:05 during a sudden traffic spike.`,`Rollout reality: you don't flip to passkeys overnight. Start with your most-targeted accounts — admins, finance, anyone on breach-notification lists. Offer passkey-first with password fallback, watch enrollment numbers, then sunset SMS. Six months later the credential-stuffing dashboards go quiet, and login-storm pages at 3 AM become someone else's vocabulary lesson.`],quote:`Passwords are replayable secrets; passkeys are origin-bound math — one gets stuffed, the other gets stumped.`,table:{headers:[`Factor`,`Phishing-resistant?`,`Operational cost`],rows:[[`Password`,`No — replayable forever`,`Low, but breach-amplified`],[`SMS OTP`,`No — SIM swap + relay`,`Carrier fees, SIM-swap support`],[`TOTP app`,`Weak — relayable in real time`,`Low; seed provisioning`],[`Push prompt`,`Weak — prompt fatigue`,`Low; users tap 'approve' reflexively`],[`Hardware key`,`Yes — origin + presence bound`,`Cost per key, lost-key recovery`],[`Passkey (synced)`,`Yes — origin-bound WebAuthn`,`Enrollment UX, cross-device flow`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`stuffing-chokepoints`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Credential stuffing: attack path and chokepoints`,caption:`The 14:00 during a blue-green cutover request path, annotated with every control that could have blunted it — rate limits, Argon2id verification, and the MFA/passkey step-up that ends the replay class.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  A["Attacker<br/>leaked credential dump"] --> B["Residential proxy pool<br/>~2,000 IPs"]
  B --> C["POST /login<br/>38k attempts / 10 min"]
  C --> D{"Rate limiter<br/>per-IP + per-account"}
  D -->|"429 + alert"| E["Sinkholed<br/>SOC paged"]
  D --> F["AuthN check<br/>Argon2id verify"]
  F -->|"fail"| G["401 +<br/>failure counter"]
  F -->|"pass"| H{"MFA / passkey<br/>step-up"}
  H -->|"fail x5"| I["Account lockout<br/>+ user email"]
  H -->|"pass"| J["Session issued<br/>+ security event"]`},{id:`oidc-login-flow`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`OIDC login: who vouches for whom`,caption:`The modern login sequence — OAuth 2.0 carrying an OIDC identity layer, a passkey challenge at the IdP, and JWT validation plus an RBAC AuthZ check at the API. AuthN and AuthZ as separate, explicit steps.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  actor U as User
  participant App as Web app (RP)
  participant IdP as Identity provider
  participant API as Backend API
  U->>App: Click "Sign in"
  App->>IdP: Redirect — auth code request, scope=openid
  IdP->>U: Passkey challenge (WebAuthn)
  U->>IdP: Sign challenge with device-bound key
  IdP->>App: Redirect with authorization code
  App->>IdP: Exchange code + client secret
  IdP-->>App: ID token (JWT) + access token
  App->>App: Verify JWT signature, aud, exp
  App->>API: API call with access token
  API->>API: AuthZ check — RBAC policy
  API-->>App: 200 OK — least-privilege data`}],takeaways:[`Split every 'auth' incident into AuthN vs AuthZ first — roughly 80% of auth pages are one, not both, and the fixes live in different code.`,`Store passwords with Argon2id (baseline ~19 MiB memory, t=2, p=1; raise memory before iterations) — SHA-256 does billions of guesses/sec and turns every leak into stuffing fuel.`,`Rate-limit login endpoints per-IP AND per-account (e.g. 5 failures / 15 min with backoff) — stuffing is distributed across thousands of IPs, so per-IP rules alone catch a small fraction of it.`,`Validate inbound trust in a fixed order — webhook HMAC before parsing the body; JWT signature → aud → exp before reading claims — one skipped check is an auth bypass.`,`Enroll passkeys for your top ~5% most-targeted accounts first (admins, finance) — origin-bound WebAuthn eliminates phishing and credential-stuffing replay for those users outright.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Cybersecurity Terms`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-cybersecurity-terms.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`}];export{e as lessons};